# Returns API <%= edit_link %>

Returns API allows Skroutz Merchants to retrieve and manage their returns.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> Returns API is currently **under development**.

Some returns need the merchant's own judgment before Skroutz can proceed — a **return
request review**, or **MRRR**. While one is pending, the merchant must **approve** it,
**reject** it with a reason, or (for a missing item) tell us they're **sending the missing
part** instead. See [Review options](#review-options) below.

Other returns need the merchant to physically inspect the returned item and report back a
**service status** — repair it, replace it, refund it, or deny the claim. While one is
pending, the merchant must submit that outcome. See [Service status](#service-status) below.

<%= partial 'partials/toc' %>

## Setup

### Obtaining an API token

In order to be able to use the API, you need to generate an **API token** from within Smart Cart settings
page in merchant's panel (Merchants > Services > [Skroutz Marketplace](https://<%= settings.merchants_domain %>/merchants/account/shop/ecommerce_details)).

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> There can only be one active **API key** per shop at a certain time.
> Creating a new API key will expire any previous one.

The same **API token** used for the [Orders API](/smart_cart/orders_api) is used here.

### Authorization

Pass in a valid **API token** in the `Authorization` header, prefixed with `Bearer `
as in the following curl example.

A specific `Accept` header is also required.

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X GET https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111111 \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here'
</pre>

## Review options

API-integrated merchants learn a return needs their attention the same way they learn about
any other return: a `merchant_return_created` or `merchant_return_updated`
[webhook](/smart_cart/returns_webhook). When a return is pending merchant review, that
webhook's payload includes a `review_options` list — every option currently valid for this
specific return — which the merchant's system uses to build its own review UI and to know
what to submit back.

There is no separate "what should I do" endpoint: `review_options` is only delivered via the
webhook payload (and via [`GET /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code`](#retrieve-a-single-return),
which returns the exact same shape, for whenever a client needs to re-fetch a return's current
state instead of waiting on a webhook).

`review_options` is present only while the return's `state` is `pending_merchant_review`, as
part of the [return object](#return-object-attributes-appendix). It's a
flat list — no nesting — of every option currently valid for **this** return; which ones appear
depends on why the customer requested the return.

Every entry always carries an `option` field. Every **reject**-kind entry (every option except
`approve` and `send_missing_part`) also always carries both of the following, explicitly `true`
or `false`:

Name | Type | Description
---- | ---- | -----------
`comment_required` | Boolean | A non-blank `comment` must be submitted with this option
`supports_proposed_part_url_required` | Boolean | A `proposed_part_url` may be submitted alongside this option

#### Example 1 (faulty-product return)

The merchant can approve, or reject with one of the four listed reasons.

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "contact_manufacturer", "comment_required": true, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false },
    { "option": "resolved_via_customer_communication", "comment_required": false, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false },
    { "option": "usability_suggestions", "comment_required": true, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false },
    { "option": "not_faulty", "comment_required": true, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Example 2 (missing-part return)

`send_missing_part` only appears when the line item isn't fulfilled by Skroutz cargo.

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "send_missing_part" },
    { "option": "resolved_via_customer_communication", "comment_required": false, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false },
    { "option": "part_not_included", "comment_required": true, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": true },
    { "option": "incorrect_skroutz_content", "comment_required": true, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Example 3 (withdrawal-policy return)

Only ever these two options; `comment_required` is `false` here because a canned comment is
filled in automatically.

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "out_of_policy_withdrawal", "comment_required": false, "supports_proposed_part_url_required": false }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

> `incorrect_skroutz_content` is the external name for what is stored internally as a
> "not missing part" rejection reason (Skroutz's own catalog data was wrong, not the
> merchant's fault) — send exactly the string shown in `review_options`, not any internal name.

## Service status

Like [review options](#review-options), a return needing a service status is announced the same
way as any other return update: a `merchant_return_created` or `merchant_return_updated`
[webhook](/smart_cart/returns_webhook). When a return is pending service inspection, that
webhook's payload includes a `service_request` object describing what the merchant can currently
submit, which the merchant's system uses to build its own service form and to know what to
submit back.

There is no separate "what should I do" endpoint: `service_request` is only delivered via the
webhook payload (and via [`GET /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code`](#retrieve-a-single-return),
which returns the exact same shape, for whenever a client needs to re-fetch a return's current
state instead of waiting on a webhook).

`service_request` is present only while the return's `state` is `pending_service_inspection`, as
part of the [return object](#return-object-attributes-appendix).

Name                    | Type    | Description
------------------------ | ------- | -----------
`service_request_id`    | Integer | ID to submit the outcome against — see [Submit a service status](#submit-a-service-status)
`service_options`       | Object  | What the merchant can currently submit — see below

`service_options` always carries an `additional_information_to_the_customer` object (a comment
and/or additional files may always be attached, regardless of the chosen option) and an `options`
array — every service outcome currently valid for **this** return. Which ones appear depends on
the returned item:

Name                                                       | Type    | Description
------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- | -----------
`options`                                                    | Array   | Every service outcome currently valid for this return — see below
`additional_information_to_the_customer.comment_optional`    | Boolean | A `comment` may optionally be submitted
`additional_information_to_the_customer.file_optional`       | Boolean | Additional files may optionally be submitted alongside the report file, via `additional_attachments`

Each element of `options` always carries an `option` field, matching the convention used by
[`review_options`](#review-options). `service_report_file_required` and
`in_stock_answer_required` are only present (and only ever `true`) when submitting that
particular option requires a `service_report_file` / `in_stock` value respectively — both are
omitted entirely (never sent as `false`) otherwise:

Name                            | Type    | Description
--------------------------------- | ------- | -----------
`option`                         | String  | The value to submit back as `option` — see [Submit a service status](#submit-a-service-status)
`service_report_file_required`   | Boolean | (Optional) A `service_report_file` must be submitted with this option
`in_stock_answer_required`       | Boolean | (Optional) An `in_stock` value must be submitted with this option

#### Example 1 (normal faulty item)

<%= render_code '{
  "service_request_id": 4821,
  "service_options": {
    "options": [
      { "option": "under_warranty_repairable", "service_report_file_required": true },
      { "option": "under_warranty_unrepairable", "in_stock_answer_required": true },
      { "option": "no_warranty_repairable", "service_report_file_required": true },
      { "option": "no_warranty_unrepairable", "service_report_file_required": true },
      { "option": "unconfirmed", "service_report_file_required": true }
    ],
    "additional_information_to_the_customer": {
      "comment_optional": true,
      "file_optional": true
    }
  }
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Example 2 (early-returned item with a refund already requested)

`under_warranty_refund` replaces the two `under_warranty_*` options above; `unconfirmed` is
always available regardless of the item.

<%= render_code '{
  "service_request_id": 4822,
  "service_options": {
    "options": [
      { "option": "under_warranty_refund" },
      { "option": "no_warranty_repairable", "service_report_file_required": true },
      { "option": "no_warranty_unrepairable", "service_report_file_required": true },
      { "option": "unconfirmed", "service_report_file_required": true }
    ],
    "additional_information_to_the_customer": {
      "comment_optional": true,
      "file_optional": true
    }
  }
}', 'javascript' %>

> `unconfirmed` means the merchant is denying the claim — the item isn't actually faulty, or
> isn't covered — rather than repairing, replacing or refunding it.

## Endpoints

### Retrieve a single return

Fetch the details of a single return, identified by its `return_code`.

<pre class="terminal">
  GET /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code
</pre>

#### Example

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X GET https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111111 \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here'
</pre>

#### Response

<%= render_code '{
  "return": {
    "return_code": "R-111111-1111111",
    "state": "returning",
    "order_code": "DEMO-OPEN",
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T10:00:00Z",
    "courier_tracking_ids": ["DEMO-RET-TRACK-1", "DEMO-RET-TRACK-2"],
    "courier": "ACS",
    "returning_address": {
      "street_name": "Demo Street",
      "street_number": "1",
      "zip": "11111",
      "city": "Demo City",
      "region": "Demo Region",
      "country_code": "GR"
    },
    "return_line_items": [
      {
        "id": "DEMO-ITEM-1",
        "ean": "1234567890123",
        "product_name": "Demo Product",
        "returning_quantity": 1,
        "return_reason": "damaged_product",
        "user_comment": "This is a demo comment explaining the return.",
        "user_photos": [
          "https://a.scdn.gr/demo/return_photo_1.jpg"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}', 'javascript' %>

### Review a return

To resolve a return that is `pending_merchant_review`, provide the `option` from that return's
current [`review_options`](#review-options). A `comment` is required when that option's
`comment_required` is `true`. You can also specify `proposed_part_url`, which is only used
when the option's `supports_proposed_part_url_required` is `true`; it's silently dropped
otherwise.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code/review
</pre>

Approving, rejecting, and sending the missing part are the same underlying decision — you can
only do one of them once. After a successful call:

* `state` moves off `pending_merchant_review` — to `returning` on approve, `rejected` on
  reject; `send_missing_part` cancels the return itself, since the customer is getting the
  missing part directly rather than a replacement/refund through the return.
* The next `merchant_return_updated` [webhook](/smart_cart/returns_webhook) reflects that change.
* `review_options` disappears from the payload — there is nothing left to resolve.

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> Unlike the equivalent action in the Merchants panel, `send_missing_part` here does **not**
> create a replacement delivery — it only cancels the return and emails the customer. The panel
> creates that delivery through a separate, much larger form (courier, pickup slot, parcel
> count, pickup address, weight, item description); the API has no equivalent input for that
> today, so a merchant using this endpoint is expected to arrange the missing part's delivery
> to the customer outside of this call.

#### Example 1 (rejecting a faulty return)

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111111/review \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{ "option": "not_faulty", "comment": "The product works within its stated specifications." }'
</pre>

#### Example 2 (approving a withdrawal return)

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111112/review \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{ "option": "approve" }'
</pre>

#### Response

<%= render_code '{
  "success": true
}', 'javascript' %>

### Submit a service status

To resolve a return that is `pending_service_inspection`, provide the `option` value from that
return's current [`service_options.options`](#service-status) as `option`.
`service_report_file` (a PDF report) and `in_stock` are each required exactly when that option's
`service_report_file_required` / `in_stock_answer_required` was `true`; submitting them when not
required, or omitting them when required, is rejected. `comment` and up to five
`additional_attachments` may always optionally be attached.

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code/service_requests/:service_request_id
</pre>

After a successful call:

* `state` moves off `pending_service_inspection` — typically to `returned`.
* The next `merchant_return_updated` [webhook](/smart_cart/returns_webhook) reflects that change.
* `service_request` disappears from the payload — there is nothing left to resolve.
* The response body carries the resolved outcome as `service_request_outcome`.

#### Example (free repair under warranty)

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111111/service_requests/4821 \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -F 'option=under_warranty_repairable' \
  -F 'in_stock=true' \
  -F 'service_report_file=@service_report.pdf'
</pre>

#### Response

<%= render_code '{
  "return": {
    "return_code": "R-111111-1111111",
    "state": "returned",
    "order_code": "DEMO-OPEN",
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T10:00:00Z",
    "return_line_items": [
      {
        "id": "DEMO-ITEM-1",
        "ean": "1234567890123",
        "product_name": "Demo Product",
        "returning_quantity": 1
      }
    ]
  },
  "service_request_outcome": {
    "state": "repaired",
    "confirmed": true,
    "under_warranty": true,
    "repairable": true
  }
}', 'javascript' %>

`service_request_outcome` reflects the resolved [outcome](#service-status):

Name             | Type    | Description
----------------- | ------- | -----------
`state`          | String  | `repaired`, `replaced`, `refunded`, `manually_handled` or `unrepaired`
`confirmed`      | Boolean | Whether the claim was accepted as valid
`under_warranty` | Boolean | Whether it was handled as an in-warranty claim
`repairable`     | Boolean | Whether the item was/could be repaired

> <i class="fa fa-exclamation-triangle"></i>
> `service_request_id` is a plain integer, not the same kind of opaque ID used for
> `return_line_items[].id` — send it back exactly as received in `service_options`.

## Error handling

In case of errors, the response will have a representative HTTP status code
in the `4xx` range. The body would contain an array of `errors`.

For example, if the requested return code does not exist (or does not belong to your shop),
you will get a `404` HTTP status code (Not Found):

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "not_found",
      "messages": [
        "Return not found"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

If you submit an invalid or stale `option` to [review a return](#review-a-return), you will
get a `422` HTTP status code (Unprocessable Entity), with the following body:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "option",
      "messages": [
        "Invalid or missing option."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

`code` is `option`, `comment`, or `return_status` — for an invalid/stale option, a missing
required comment, or the return having moved out of `pending_merchant_review` in the meantime,
respectively. If the option itself was valid but the decision still failed a model-level check
(e.g. a `comment` over 1000 characters), `code` names the failing attribute instead (e.g.
`rejection_comment_sanitized`).

If you submit an invalid `option` to [submit a service status](#submit-a-service-status)
— not currently one of the `option`s in `service_options.options` — or omit a `service_report_file` /
`in_stock` value that option required, you will get a `422` HTTP status code (Unprocessable
Entity), with a single combined message under `code: "service_request_error"`:

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "service_request_error",
      "messages": [
        "Report file δεν πρέπει να είναι κενό"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

If the `service_request_id` doesn't exist, doesn't belong to this return, or was already
resolved (by you or concurrently by someone else), you get a `404` with `code: "service_request_error"`
and the message `"Service request not found"` — the same as an unknown/foreign `return_code`
gets you `code: "return_error"` with `"Return not found"`.

